1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,200
Ripple is an incident co-pilot
for Hong Kong traffic operators.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:11,000
This is archived playback of a May fifteenth
incident on Wong Chuk Hang Road flyover.

3
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:17,000
The arrow marks the approximate reported area.
The message is from the Transport Department.

4
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:23,000
We can rewind to observations
from before that report was published.

5
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:29,000
The road curves provide map context.
They are not recommended routes.

6
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:37,000
The map shows recorded detector conditions.
Missing speed is not a speed of zero.

7
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:45,000
These three detectors have partial archive coverage.
Their counts do not tell us which detour drivers took.

8
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:52,000
The later TD update reports all lanes closed.
The callout changes as playback reaches the report.

9
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:59,000
Publication time does not establish
the exact physical closure time.

10
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:05,500
Later, the console shows the clearance report
alongside the recorded conditions.

11
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:11,000
These remain partial samples,
not continuous monitoring or live traffic.

12
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:18,500
Plan comparison is waiting for validated runs.
We are not presenting a recommended route yet.

13
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:25,500
The goal is to compare operator responses
and explain their measured trade-offs.

14
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:32,000
The next step is a jointly reviewed run bundle.
Benefit figures remain not measured yet.
